useReducer for Complex Local
Transitions
Volume 06 - Professional Frontend | Phase 23

Reducer is not a global store
useReducer is valuable when one local feature has several related transitions and scattered setters make
the rules difficult to audit. It centralizes transition logic without changing state ownership.

Bulk selection example
type SelectionState = { ids: Set<string> }

type Action =
| { type: "toggle"; id: string }
| { type: "replace"; ids: string[] }
| { type: "clear" }

function selectionReducer(
state: SelectionState,
action: Action,
): SelectionState {
// explicit transitions live here
}

Use reducer when
• Several events modify the same local state model.

• Transitions have invariants worth naming.

• You want pure transition tests.

• The state still belongs to one feature/component boundary.

Do not use reducer merely because
• The component has more than one useState call.

• The data came from the server.

• You want to avoid learning the actual owner of the state.

Derived State, Selectors and
Computation
Volume 06 - Professional Frontend | Phase 23

If it can be computed, prefer computing it
Derived state is information that can be calculated from authoritative state during render or through a
memoized selector when the computation is actually expensive.

Examples
const tasks = tasksQuery.data?.data ?? []

const completedCount = tasks.filter(
task => task.status === "DONE",
).length

const hasSelection = selectedIds.size > 0

const canGoNext = Boolean(tasksQuery.data?.meta.hasNextPage)

Avoid synchronized counters
// Avoid
const [completedCount, setCompletedCount] = useState(0)

useEffect(() => {
setCompletedCount(
tasks.filter(task => task.status === "DONE").length
)
}, [tasks])

Memoization rule
Use useMemo for measured or structurally useful expensive computation, not as a ritual
around every derived value. Correct ownership matters more than premature memoization.

Server/Client Boundaries and State
Placement
Volume 06 - Professional Frontend | Phase 23

Client state creates a client boundary
In the App Router, Server Components are still the default architecture. Introducing useState,
useReducer, browser hooks, or interactive event handlers requires a Client Component boundary. The
goal is not to avoid clients; it is to keep interaction boundaries narrow.

Example split
// Server Component
export default async function TasksPage({ searchParams }) {
const query = normalize(await searchParams)
return <TasksClientRegion query={query} />
}

// Client Component
"use client"
